  "type": "answer",
  "content": "The root cause of the latency is **memory tiering contention** (NVMe wait) on the hosts of CL-SG-PRD-PAY, specifically triggered by a \"warm-up\" period following the National Day long weekend.\n\n**Mechanism and Reason**\nThe L1 compute's premise that the system is swapping is wrong. \"Highest ESXi DRAM Memory Consumed\" of 157% is a design feature of memory tiering, where pages exceed DRAM and reside in NVMe [`vsphere/object-types/cluster/models/cluster-risk`]. The metrics prove there is no swapping or ballooning (both 0), so there is no \"swap-in\" contention [`vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/virtual-motherboard/swapped`].\n\nThe latency is caused by **NVMe wait**. On `esxi-pay-05`, the NVMe read bandwidth is high (380 MBps) and NVMe read latency has spiked to 290 µs. This is a classic \"warm-up\" scenario: after the long weekend, pages that went cold and moved to NVMe are being fetched back into DRAM [`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`]. The \"Worst memory wait\" of 5.8% confirms this is the primary performance hit, as it sums memory latency and NVMe wait [`vsphere/object-types/cluster/cluster-performance`].\n\n**What to check next**\n1. **Wait for warm-up**: Compare current NVMe read bandwidth against values from the previous business day to see if it is falling.\n2. **Check NVMe device health**: Run `esxcli nvme device log smart get` to ensure the latency is not due to hardware wear [`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`].\n3. **Verify Tiering exclusion**: Confirm if any high-priority VMs should have tiering disabled via full memory reservations [`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`].",
