 "turn_2": {
  "type": "answer",
  "content": "CHG-2 caused the latency. No, do not roll back CHG-2.\n\n**Mechanism and Reason**\nCHG-1 disabled tiering and added full reservations for six SQL VMs (6 x 256 GB = 1.536 TB). Because full reservations for non-tiered VMs shrink the DRAM available for other VMs (`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`), the effective DRAM:NVMe ratio for the rest of the cluster fell significantly.\n\nCHG-2 then added 40 VMs (40 x 32 GB = 1.28 TB). While the cluster had \"Capacity Remaining\" (which counts total usable capacity, including the NVMe tier), it did not have enough DRAM. This forced more pages of the tiered VMs into the NVMe tier, as seen in the \"Highest ESXi DRAM Memory Consumed\" rising from 121% to 157% and \"Memory Tiering Consumption\" increasing from 21% to 44% [`vsphere/object-types/cluster/models/cluster-risk`, `vsphere/object-types/cluster/models/cluster-consumption`].\n\nThe latency occurred on Tuesday because the working set of these VMs now exceeds the reduced DRAM. Following the long weekend, a \"warm-up\" happened: pages that moved to NVMe were fetched back, causing high NVMe read bandwidth (380 MBps) and NVMe wait (Worst memory wait 5.8%) [`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`, `vsphere/object-types/cluster/cluster-performance`].\n\n**What to check next**\nCheck if the \"Worst ESXi Active Memory\" (63.5%) stays above 50% as the morning progresses; if it does not fall, the working set no longer fits in DRAM, and you must move some VMs to other clusters [`vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`].",
